# Q10 round-2 report patch (idempotent): python3 -I build_report_q10v2.py  -- replaces all Q10* results
import json,shutil
p='/output/report.json'; shutil.copy(p,'/workspace/out/report.backup.q10r2pre.json'); r=json.load(open(p))
r['results']=[x for x in r['results'] if not x['quantity'].startswith('Q10')]
A=lambda n,v:{"name":n,"value":v}
com=[A("data","Xb4b (0.30\" tapered) / Xb4d (1.05\") Band 6 HCN/HCO+ 3-2 cubes (pbcor) and 258 GHz line-free continuum; 2016.1.00965.S spw29 Band 3 cube+mfs (0.67x0.56\" PA 66, 98.05 GHz; H40a at 98.11 GHz; channel 24.1 km/s; channel rms 0.3 mJy/beam)"),
 A("convention","moment-0 +-350 km/s about VSYS=2780 km/s, linear baseline; matched circular beams (0.30\", 1.05\"); annuli about each nucleus' HCO+ peak (N (-0.15,+2.55\"), S (-0.15,-2.55\")); pixels beam-spaced; line fluxes in Jy/beam km/s, continuum in mJy/beam"),
 A("H40a free-free L/C","LTE, optically thin: FWHM*T_L/T_C = 6.985e3 nu_GHz^1.1 Te^-1.15/(1+y+) km/s (Rohlfs & Wilson / Gordon & Sorochenko), y+=0.08; x1.0645 for the Gaussian integral; = 26.8 km/s (integral; 25.2 FWHM*peak) at 98.11 GHz, Te=1e4 K; 49 (5000 K) ... 21.8 (1.2e4 K); b_n*beta_n=1 assumed")]
def add(q,t,v,u,un,c,e): r['results'].append({"quantity":q,"target":t,"value":v,"uncertainty":un,"unit":u,"confidence":c,"evidence":e,"assumptions":com})
add("Q10 (answer): the N inward rise of HCN/HCO+ 3-2 is a property of the N nucleus, not a function of continuum surface brightness: it saturates at the thick/thermalised value R~1 at the core, follows the dust continuum only within N, and S stays flat although its 1.1 mm continuum is 40-65% free-free/synchrotron",
 "N and S nuclei, 0.30\" and 1.05\"",
 "(1) N: R=0.98/0.87/0.45/0.30 for r=0-0.15/0.25-0.5/1-1.5/2-3\" (0.30\" beam), 0.805/0.726/0.599/0.474/0.399 at 1.05\"; S: R=0.42/0.41/0.40/0.36/0.33 (0.30\"), 0.39/0.36/0.36/0.39 (1.05\") while its continuum falls 100x. (2) Pooled N+S pixels: once a nucleus indicator is included, R has no significant dependence on log continuum, log HCO+ or radius (0.30\": std. coeff on log Sc +0.05 [+0.02,+0.09] against a shifted-continuum null of 0.00+-0.05; nucleus term -0.14 [-0.19,-0.08]; 1.05\": log Sc +0.01 [-0.05,+0.06], nucleus -0.18 [-0.22,-0.13], R^2 0.70 vs 0.32 without it). At equal HCO+ brightness N and S differ (0.40-0.80 Jy/b km/s: R=0.63+-0.04 vs 0.43+-0.04; 0.30\"). (3) Within N alone R rises with continuum at 0.30\" (std. slope +0.08 [+0.06,+0.10], null -0.01+-0.04; partially degenerate with radius), S alone: none. (4) Velocity-resolved R is flat across the line (N 1.01+-0.03/1.01+-0.01/0.97+-0.02 blue/core/red; S 0.43+-0.01/0.39+-0.02/0.36+-0.05): no self-absorption/opacity or wing signature. (5) RADEX: R from 0.4 to 1.0 at fixed 4-3/3-2 ladders needs X=N(HCN)/N(HCO+) up by x3-4 (log X +0.5-0.6) -- or an HCO+ deficit of the same size; density (>=2 dex) or column (>=1 dex) changes cannot do it without raising the HCN 4-3/3-2 ratio to 0.8-0.9 (observed 0.39 N, 0.27 S). R~1 at N core is the thermalised/thick ceiling.",
 "ratio","see sub-entries Q10a-Q10h","medium","scripts/q10_reg.py, q10_h40a_v2.py, q10_decomp.py, q10_radex.py -> out/q10v2/*.log")
add("Q10a: radial N/S profiles and the individual lines: in N the HCO+ follows the 1.1 mm continuum but HCN falls ~2.5x faster; in S both lines are far flatter than the continuum",
 "N and S nuclei, 0.30\"",
 "Normalised to the r<0.15\" core, for r=0.15-0.25/0.25-0.5/0.5-1/1-1.5/1.5-2\": N HCN 0.73/0.46/0.22/0.066/0.027, HCO+ 0.75/0.52/0.33/0.145/0.063, continuum 0.80/0.65/0.41/0.15/0.068. HCO+/continuum is constant (2.2,2.1,1.7,1.8,2.1,2.0 Jy km/s per mJy) while HCN/continuum falls 2.13->0.84: the N rise of R is HCN concentration (steeper than both HCO+ and dust), not an HCO+ deficit at the centre. S: HCN 0.75/0.40/0.13/0.049/0.038, HCO+ 0.77/0.42/0.155/0.063/0.043, continuum 0.58/0.19/0.047/0.024/0.006 -- the S continuum is 3-6x more centrally concentrated than the lines (AGN/free-free core), and R is flat. Core HCN/HCO+ (Jy/b km/s): N 1.353/1.375, S 0.514/1.217; core continuum N 0.635, S 1.631 mJy/b: at ~equal HCO+ (1.4 vs 1.2) S has 2.6x more continuum and 0.43x the R.",
 "ratio","profile errors ~0.02-0.03 (R) at 0.30\"; annuli not independent","medium","scripts/q10_reg.py -> out/q10v2/reg.log")
add("Q10b: continuum subtraction and HCN v2=1 do not drive the N rise or S flatness (reworded)",
 "N and S cores, 0.30\"",
 "Core r<0.15\" R(N)=0.984 (linear baseline) / 0.976 (constant); R(S)=0.423 / 0.426. The reviewer's independent baseline fits (|v|>520/700/900 km/s, team/checks/rev_q10.py) give residual baselines 0.00-0.05 mJy/beam (the cubes are already continuum-subtracted), vs core continuum 0.64 (N), 1.63 (S) mJy/b: no continuum-subtraction artefact. (Putting the continuum back in gives R(S)=0.70 -- an artificial test, not a measurement.) HCN v2=1f 3-2 (+401 km/s in the HCO+ cube): N -5.8+-14.7, S -2.8+-14.7 mJy/b km/s at 0.30\", i.e. <3% (3 sigma) of HCN v=0; Q4 limits at native/1.05\": N <0.059/<0.030, S <0.080/<0.048 => no IR-pumping signature, and IR pumping would raise R preferentially where the continuum is strong, which S (strongest continuum, lowest R) contradicts.",
 "ratio","3-sigma <=3% of HCN v=0","medium","scripts/q10_corr.py -> out/q10/q10_corr.log; team/checks/rev_q10.py")
add("Q10c (revised): H40a is consistent with pure free-free: matched/stacked L/C = 17+-11 (N) and 20+-8 (S) km/s at 1.05\" vs 26.8 km/s expected; S marginally detected (2.4-3.3 sigma), N 1.5 sigma; f_ff(98 GHz) = 0.63+-0.41 (N), 0.74+-0.31 (S) for Te=1e4 K",
 "N and S nuclei, 2016.1.00965.S Band 3",
 "Expected free-free H40a integral/continuum (Rohlfs&Wilson): 49/35/26.8/21.8 km/s for Te=5000/8000/1e4/1.2e4 K (earlier hard-coded 3.3 km/s was 8x too low). Matched-filter extraction (velocity-registered on the HCN centroid field, Gaussian template FWHM fixed to the registered HCN line 160-190 km/s, noise from 60 off-source apertures and from line-free velocity shifts, the larger used): 1.05\" beam, r<1\": N I=21.0+-13.7 mJy/b km/s (1.5 sigma), C=1.24 mJy/b, L/C=16.9+-11.0 km/s; S 32.3+-13.6 (2.4 sigma), C=1.62, L/C=19.9+-8.4. Native 0.67x0.56\", r<0.5\": N 17.8+-11.3 (1.6 sigma), L/C 21.9+-13.9; S 40.7+-12.3 (3.3 sigma), L/C 24.2+-7.3. f_ff(98)=(L/C)meas/(L/C)ff: N 0.63+-0.41 (Te 5000 K 0.28+-0.18; 1.2e4 K 0.78+-0.51); S 0.74+-0.31 (0.33+-0.14; 0.92+-0.39); native S r<0.5\" 0.90+-0.27. Compared with Michiyama+2020 (ApJ 895, 85, Table 6; beam 1.48x1.31\", cube rms 0.08 mJy/b, integrated rms 11 mJy/b km/s): H40a 273+-14 (N) and 143+-7 (S) mJy km/s, L/C 43+-3 (Te=5900 K, f_ff~0.76) and 24+-2 km/s (Te=10200 K, f_ff~0.90). Our 1.5\"-beam fluxes inside r<2.5\": N 109+-50, S 166+-86 mJy km/s (N 1.6 sigma below, S consistent); our noise is ~4-8x theirs, so a deeper dataset detects it and ours is consistent with it: the Michiyama-expected total L/C (0.76x46=35, 0.90x25=22 km/s) vs ours 17+-11, 20+-8. Aperture gain over the old single-beam +-350 km/s windows is only ~1.2-1.4x (noise is correlated over a beam, so r<1\" is ~1 beam); the registration and window match matter little (unregistered integrals are within 15%).",
 "mJy/beam km/s; km/s","1-sigma from off-source apertures + line-free shifts; Te 5000-12000 K; b_n beta_n = 1 (+-10%)","low","scripts/q10_h40a_v2.py -> out/q10v2/h40a_v2.log, h40a_v2.json")
add("Q10d (revised): 3-component (free-free + synchrotron + dust) decomposition of the 98-258 GHz continuum: N is 70-80% dust at 1.1 mm at all radii, the S core only 35-45% dust (free-free ~35-60%, synchrotron/AGN 5-20%)",
 "N and S annuli at 1.05\" matched beam",
 "Spectral index alpha(98-258): N 0.92+-0.09/1.06+-0.09/1.36+-0.13/1.91+-0.30 (0-0.5/0.5-1/1-1.5/1.5-2\"), S 0.28+-0.08/0.44+-0.10/0.91+-0.19/1.76+-0.50. Monte Carlo (20000 draws; a_ff=-0.1, a_sync U(-0.9,-0.5), a_dust U(3.5,4.0), f_ff(98) constant in r; 5%+5% flux scale + noise): scenario A (f_ff from our H40a with Te 5000-12000 K) dust fraction at 258 GHz N 0.73 [0.68,0.78]/0.76/0.83/0.91, S 0.45 [0.36,0.54]/0.54/0.71/0.90; ff 0.16 and 0.37, sync 0.11 and 0.18 in the cores; scenario B (Michiyama f_ff 0.76 N, 0.90 S): dust N 0.68/0.72/0.79/0.87, S 0.35 [0.29,0.41]/0.45/0.65/0.80, sync 0.04-0.05 in the cores. Dust 1.1 mm surface brightness (mJy/b, scenario A/B): N 3.18/2.96, 2.24/2.11, 1.22/1.17, 0.64/0.61; S 1.70/1.33, 0.97/0.82, 0.48/0.43, 0.33/0.29. R vs dust SB: N dR/dSB_dust=+0.13-0.14 per mJy/b, S +0.01 (flat). At the S core dust SB (1.3-1.7) N interpolates to R=0.62-0.66 vs S 0.39: R is not a single function of dust SB either. 6-26% (A) / 5-73% (B, outer S) of the draws rejected as unphysical (f_s<0): the outer-S decomposition is unreliable.",
 "fraction; mJy/beam","fractions: MC 16-84%; alpha stat 0.08-0.5; f_ff(98) assumed constant with radius (H40a gives no radial information)","low","scripts/q10_decomp.py -> out/q10v2/decomp.log; alpha inputs out/q10/q10_alpha.json")
add("Q10e: uv matching of the 98/258 GHz continuum: Xb4d recovers ~87% (Xb4b 73%) of the compact-array 258 GHz central flux at 6\"; alpha in the outer annuli is the least safe",
 "central 6\" region",
 "Continuum smoothed to 6\" circular, central value: Xb4f (5.8x3.5\") 22.0 mJy/6\"-beam, Xb4d 19.2 (87%), Xb4b 16.2 (73%), 98 GHz (2016.1.00965.S) 5.77; 258 flux in r<3\" 19.5 (Xb4b), 21.2 (Xb4d), 16.4 (Xb4f; uncorrected aperture sum). The 98 GHz data's uv coverage (MRS) is not recorded in the image products, so a common-uv alpha cannot be computed in the image plane; the 1.05\" smoothing removes most of the resolution difference but extended flux resolved out at 98 GHz would flatten alpha; the effect is largest at r>1.5\" where the continuum is <5% of the peak.",
 "fraction","~10-15% flux-scale/recovery systematic at 1.05\"","low","scripts/q10_reg.py -> out/q10v2/reg.log")
add("Q10f: velocity-resolved HCN/HCO+ at the cores is flat: no opacity/self-absorption signature and no S outflow-side change at 0.30\"",
 "N and S cores r<0.25\", 0.30\"",
 "Bins of 40 km/s (bin noise 0.06-0.07 mJy/b): N R=0.91/0.95/1.03/0.99/0.95/0.93/1.02 at v-VSYS=-120..+120; windows blue 40-200 1.01+-0.03, core 1.01+-0.01, red 40-200 0.97+-0.02. S R=0.42/0.42/0.43/0.42/0.39 at v-VSYS=-120..+40 (0.25 and 0.67 in the noisy +80/+120 bins); blue 40-200 0.43+-0.01, core 0.39+-0.02, red 40-200 0.36+-0.05; wings beyond 200 km/s undetected (HCN 0.03+-0.04 mJy/b). Peak velocities N +48/+61, S -58/-66 (HCN/HCO+) km/s rel. 2780.",
 "ratio","per bin +-0.01-0.05","medium","scripts/q10_reg.py -> out/q10v2/reg.log")
add("Q10g: RADEX consequences (LAMDA, Tbg 2.73 K, dv 150 km/s): what each interpretation predicts for R vs continuum and for the ladders",
 "N and S, grid Tk 20-150 K, log n 3-7, N(HCO+) 1e14-1e17, X 0.3-30",
 "R32 (Tb ratio) saturates at ~1 for thick, thermalised gas (e.g. Tk=30, N(HCO+)=1e16.5, n>=1e6: R=0.82-1.0 even for X=3), so the N core R=0.93-1.0 sits at the ceiling; it cannot rise further with continuum. Reference S-like state (Tk 30-80, log n=4, N(HCO+)=1e16, X=3): R=0.29-0.40. Single-parameter moves to R~1.0: (i) abundance ratio X x3-4 (X 3->10): R=0.97-1.22 with 4-3/3-2 unchanged (HCN 0.23-0.39, HCO+ 0.35-0.47) -- matches the observed similar N/S ladders (HCN 0.39/0.27, HCO+ 0.48/0.44); (ii) density 1e4->1e6.5: R=0.4->0.8-1.0 but HCN 4-3/3-2 -> 0.78-0.9 (excluded by 0.39); (iii) column N(HCO+) 1e16->1e17: R only 0.29->0.43 (Tk=30), 0.40->0.52 (80 K); (iv) IR pumping/Tbg: dust Tb at 355 GHz 0.3 (N)/1.0 K (S) -> Tbg,eff 3.0/3.7 K, R changes <few %, v2=1f <3-6%. => abundance (X(HCN)/X(HCO+) up by ~0.5-0.6 dex, equivalently HCO+ depleted/destroyed in N) is the only single change that explains R without changing ladders; HCN and HCO+ individually: HCO+ ∝ continuum, HCN concentrates (Q10a). With CS 7-6/HCO+ N/S = 2.1+-0.3 but CS/HCN 1.1+-0.2 (Q14e, 4-6\" beams) N is HCO+-poor rather than HCN-rich relative to S. Not discriminated: starburst chemistry (mechanical heating/cosmic rays: would make R follow SFR density, which is not seen across nuclei), XDR/AGN (S has the AGN, but lowest R, so an X-ray/AGN-driven HCN enhancement is disfavoured at S); HCO+ destruction by higher electron fraction/lower ionisation in the dense N core is allowed.",
 "ratio","grid steps 0.5 dex in n, 1 dex in N; ladders from Q1b","low","scripts/q10_radex.py -> out/q10v2/radex.log")
add("Q10h: pixel-level partial dependence (replaces the underpowered Spearman tests): pooled and per nucleus, with nulls",
 "N and S, 0.30\" (n=85 beam-spaced pixels, 36 grid offsets) and 1.05\" (n=23)",
 "Model R ~ logSc + logHCO+ + r (+ nucleus indicator), standardised coefficients (<R^2>): 0.30\" without indicator: 0.08/-0.08/-0.03 (0.10); with: 0.06/-0.09/-0.06 and nucleus -0.16 (0.23), bootstrap 16-84%: logSc +0.016..+0.092, logHCO+ -0.155..-0.052, r -0.089..-0.041, nucleus -0.19..-0.08; null (continuum shifted 3-8 beams, 292 shifts) for logSc: 0.00+-0.05. 1.05\": without indicator R^2 0.34 (logSc 0.08), with: 0.70, logSc -0.02 [-0.05,+0.06], nucleus -0.20 [-0.22,-0.13]; null 0.00+-0.02 (n=3 shifts only). Per nucleus 0.30\": N slope on logSc +0.08 [+0.06,+0.10] (null -0.01+-0.04), partial +0.13 [+0.09,+0.18]; S slope -0.11 [-0.16,-0.07], partial +0.04 [-0.03,+0.10]; 1.05\" (n=12/10): N +0.10, partial +0.02 [-0.03,+0.06]; S 0.00. Conclusion: at 0.30\" N shows a ~2-sigma continuum dependence within the nucleus (degenerate with radius); the pooled data are dominated by the N-S offset at 1.05\".",
 "std. coefficients","bootstrap 16-84% over pixels; beam-spaced pixels only","medium","scripts/q10_reg.py -> out/q10v2/reg.log")
r['recipe'].append({'order':len(r['recipe'])+1,'stage':'statistical_analysis','description':'Q10 round 2: (1) q10_h40a_v2.py: free-free H40a L/C formula, velocity-registered matched-filter extraction in matched apertures at native/1.05"/1.5" with empirical noise, f_ff, Michiyama+2020 comparison; (2) q10_decomp.py: Monte Carlo 3-component continuum decomposition per annulus; (3) q10_reg.py: pooled/per-nucleus regression with shifted-continuum nulls, individual line profiles, velocity-resolved ratios, uv-recovery check; (4) q10_radex.py: RADEX consequences; (5) build_report_q10v2.py','scripts':['scripts/q10_h40a_v2.py','scripts/q10_decomp.py','scripts/q10_reg.py','scripts/q10_radex.py','scripts/build_report_q10v2.py'],'software':'numpy, scipy, astropy, RADEX'})
